# November 17, 2026: Northwest Connecticut (Litchfield County), first real pass.
# Chosen because the region had 3 places while the Berkshires next door had 9 and the Hudson Valley 22,
# and it is about 1.5 hours from Albany.
# Facts come only from the linked sources, each opened in this session: Wikipedia articles read as raw text
# (tools/wiki_raw.py), Connecticut DEEP and ctparks.com park pages, the CT DECD museum page, the Steep Rock
# Association, the 1979 National Register nomination for Sharon Valley, ConnecticutHistory.org, the Lakeville
# Journal, the AMC Connecticut chapter, FirstLight, WillhiteWeb and two hikers' sites (CTMQ, Damned CT).
# Where sources disagree the text gives both and conf is 'mixed'. Where something was not checked, it says so.
# Every pin was looked at on the Esri satellite with tools/pincheck.py; the pin notes say what it showed.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
DEEP_OTHER=L('CT DEEP: Other state parks and forests','https://portal.ct.gov/deep/state-parks/locate-park-forest/other-state-parks-and-forests')
C=[
# ---- iron country ----
N('kent-iron-furnace','Kent Iron Furnace','Kent','ct','industry','ruins',41.73861,-73.47,'site','public','walk','','1826–1892',
  'A blast furnace on the Housatonic that made pig iron from 1826, mostly for railroads, from ore quarried in South Kent. It began as a cold-blast furnace, was rebuilt as a hot-blast furnace in 1846 and enlarged to its present size in 1870. It ran until 1892.',
  'The granite stack, laid without mortar and held with iron plates and tie rods, with pointed-arch openings on three faces. It stands down the hill from the Eric Sloane Museum, on a rise above the river.',
  'A seventy-year iron furnace in the yard of a tool museum.',
  'On the grounds of the Eric Sloane Museum, 31 Kent-Cornwall Road (US 7), north of Kent village. The state owns and runs it (State Historic Preservation Office); the state calls the site a State Archaeological Preserve, so leave everything as found. The grounds are reached over the tracks of the Housatonic Railroad, which is an active railroad: cross only at the crossing. Opening hours were not checked; see the state page.',
  [W('Eric Sloane Museum'),L('CT DECD: Eric Sloane Museum and Kent Iron Furnace','https://portal.ct.gov/DECD/Content/Historic-Preservation/04_State_Museums/Eric-Sloane-Museum-and-Kent-Iron-Furnace')]),
N('mine-hill-roxbury','Mine Hill iron works','Roxbury','ct','industry','ruins',41.56083,-73.33528,'site','public','hike','','1865–1905',
  'Settlers dug shafts into the hill in the mid-1700s looking for gold, silver and lead; none of it lasted. Carbonated iron ore was found here in the early 1800s, but disputes over ownership held mining back until 1865, when the Shepaug Spathic Iron and Steel Company bought the hill. It built mines, a railroad carrying ore down to furnaces at the foot of the hill, a small workers\' village and a reservoir on Mineral Spring Brook. A granite quarry opened later and shipped stone by rail to New York City. The iron works closed in 1905 and the quarry in 1935.',
  'The preserved remains of the iron works, with signs explaining each part; 18th-century shafts and abandoned quarries on the longer loop; the old reservoir site.',
  'A whole iron-making complex, furnaces to mine shafts, on one hillside.',
  'Mine Hill Preserve, owned by the Roxbury Land Trust; open dawn to dusk. Parking area on Mine Hill Road. A 0.2-mile loop goes to the blast furnaces, a 3.5-mile loop through the mining areas, and a 0.3-mile trail to the reservoir site. Old mine shafts: stay on the trails and out of any opening.',
  [W('Mine Hills Preserve'),L('Roxbury Land Trust: Mine Hill Preserve event page','https://www.roxburylandtrust.org/mine-hill-preserve')]),
N('sharon-valley-lime-kiln','Sharon Valley lime kiln and furnace site','Sharon','ct','industry','ruins',41.88791,-73.49661,'site','view','drive','','1825–c.1900',
  'Lyman Bradley built Sharon\'s first blast furnace here in 1825, by Webatuck Creek. The Sharon Valley Iron Company ran it later, went to hot blast in 1863, and probably ran the lime kiln as a sideline. The kiln and furnace shut down around 1900. Who bought the works in 1898 is given two ways: Barnum & Morehouse (Wikipedia) and Barnum, Richardson & Company (the 1979 National Register nomination).',
  'In 1979 the kiln stood about 20 feet tall and 18 feet square at the base, of rough limestone blocks banded with oak timbers, with arched openings lined with firebrick; it was crumbling at the top. Across the creek the furnace was nearly gone: limestone rubble, iron tie-rods, firebrick arches, a wheelpit and slag. How much stands today was not checked.',
  '',
  'The kiln stands on Sharon Station Road at Webatuck Creek, near the town garage and ball fields; in 1979 it sat on a one-acre lot owned by the Town of Sharon. Look from the road. The furnace remains across the creek were on private land in 1979: view only, or ask.',
  [W('Sharon Valley Historic District'),L('National Register nomination, Sharon Valley Historic District (1979)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/82004478_text')],conf='mixed'),
N('falls-village-canal','Falls Village canal of 1851','Canaan','ct','canal','traces',41.9575,-73.3685,'area','view','walk','canal','1845–1851',
  'In 1845 two iron makers, Lee Canfield and Samuel Robbins, formed the Water Company and began a three-level canal to carry water from the Great Falls of the Housatonic to mills in the village. The walls were laid without cement. When the gates were opened in 1851 the canal leaked, and it never worked. An earlier plan of 1822, for a canal to carry iron to tidewater, also came to nothing.',
  'Massive dry-laid stone walls of the unused canal, between Water Street and the railroad tracks. The power company uses about 1,900 feet of the old line today. The Appalachian Trail passes beside the canal.',
  'A canal built to make an industrial town, which leaked on opening day.',
  'See it from the Appalachian Trail, which passes beside it, and from FirstLight\'s public Falls Village Recreation Area on Water Street (parking, picnic area, interpretive trails). The federal protected-areas map shows no public land at the pin, and who owns the canal strip was not checked: stay on the trail. A path below the railroad underpass is barred by a yellow gate: do not go past it. The Housatonic Railroad tracks are active: stay off them.',
  [W('Falls Village District'),L('Lakeville Journal: Tracing Canaan\'s canals that never came to be (2025)','https://lakevillejournal.com/tracing-canaans-canals-that-never-came-to-be'),L('AMC Connecticut: History of the AT','https://ct-amc.org/?p=898'),L('FirstLight: Falls Village Recreation Area','https://www.firstlightpower.com/stewardship/falls-village-recreation-area')]),
N('canaan-union-depot','Canaan Union Depot','North Canaan','ct','rail','standing',42.02611,-73.32944,'exact','view','drive','stations','1872–1971',
  'A union station of 1872, built in the angle where the Housatonic Railroad crossed the Connecticut Western (later Central New England) line, with a three-storey telegraph tower at the corner and a restaurant inside. Passenger trains on the east-west line ended in 1927 and on the north-south line in 1971. Arson destroyed more than half the building, the south-east half, on October 12, 2001. The Connecticut Railroad Historical Association bought what was left in 2003 and restored it.',
  'The restored station and its corner tower. A brewery opened in it in December 2018 and closed at the end of 2024; an accordion museum moved in in July 2021. Who is in it now was not checked.',
  'A junction station that survived arson.',
  'Look from the street in Canaan village. The Housatonic Railroad tracks beside it are active: stay off them. Opening times of anything inside were not checked.',
  [W('Canaan Union Depot')]),
N('terryville-waterwheel','Terryville Waterwheel','Plymouth','ct','industry','standing',41.68006,-73.01526,'exact','view','drive','','1851–c.1940',
  'Eli Terry\'s clock factory was built on this spot in 1824. It burned in 1851 and the Lewis Lock Company rebuilt it, and the present wheel probably dates from then. The wheel drove lock-making until about 1940, when the factory was torn down. It is the best preserved of Connecticut\'s three surviving 19th-century waterwheels.',
  'A wooden and iron wheel in its rubblestone wheel well under a fitted shelter of 1951, on the north side of Main Street (US 6). Most of its wood was replaced in 1990–91.',
  '',
  'Seen from Main Street in Terryville. Who owns it was not checked.',
  [W('Terryville Waterwheel')]),
# ---- state parks and forests ----
N('northfield-knife-company','Northfield Knife Company ruins','Litchfield','ct','industry','ruins',41.6991,-73.10338,'site','public','walk','','1858–1919',
  'Humaston Brook was dammed in 1854 to power a carriage maker. J. Howard Catlin and his brother Franklin bought the works in 1858 for a knife company, which became one of the country\'s leading knife makers and showed its knives at world\'s fairs from 1876 to 1901. The Catlins sold it in 1919 and the state bought the property in 1926. The one-acre site of the factory remains was put on the National Register in 1997.',
  'Factory foundations along the brook below the dam (the pond is known locally as Knife Shop Pond), and cascades in the gorge.',
  '',
  'Humaston Brook State Park, CT DEEP; undeveloped, no parking fee. A trail south of Knife Shop Road leads to the cascades and the ruins. DEEP\'s directions: from Route 254 in Northfield take Knife Shop Road 0.2 mile to the dam; for the trail turn left on Newton Road, left on White Road, and park there. Hours were not checked.',
  [W('Humaston Brook State Park'),DEEP_OTHER]),
N('borden-condensed-milk','Gail Borden\'s condensed milk factory site','Torrington','ct','industry','ruins',41.87298,-73.09126,'area','public','walk','','1857–1877',
  'Milo Burr dammed several streams in 1851 to make Burr Pond and power a tannery and three sawmills. Gail Borden made condensed milk here from 1857. The sources put it differently: Wikipedia says he used one of Burr\'s buildings from 1857 to 1861 and calls it his first commercially successful factory; the state calls it the world\'s first condensed milk factory and says he built it. The building burned in 1877.',
  'Stone foundations off Burr Mountain Road (Wikipedia), and a bronze tablet that marks the factory\'s spot (the state).',
  'Where condensed milk for the Union Army began.',
  'Burr Pond State Park, CT DEEP, 384 Burr Mountain Road; open daily 8 a.m. to sunset. Free for Connecticut cars; out-of-state cars pay $15 on weekends and holidays and $10 on weekdays. The Walcott Trail, a 2.5-mile loop, goes round the pond.',
  [W('Burr Pond State Park'),W('Paugnut State Forest'),L('CT State Parks: Burr Pond State Park','https://ctparks.com/parks/burr-pond-state-park')],conf='mixed'),
N('lovers-leap-ruins','Lovers Leap factory ruins and iron bridge','New Milford','ct','industry','ruins',41.54315,-73.40485,'area','public','walk','','1895',
  'A small state park on the Housatonic gorge, made from the estate of Catherine Judson Hurd, who left 52 acres to the state in 1971, and land on the west side bought from Connecticut Light and Power; it was dedicated in 2007. Its history includes the factory of the Bridgeport Wood Finishing Company and the Hurd estate. The wrought-iron lenticular truss bridge was built in 1895 by the Berlin Iron Bridge Company, one of its last in iron; it carried cars until 1977 and is now for walkers only. When the factory ran and what it made were not found in the sources read.',
  'Factory ruins, the remains of an old estate (Wikipedia names a vine-covered "yellow cat tea house") and other traces of industry in the north-west part of the park near the water; the iron bridge 100 feet above the gorge.',
  '',
  'Lovers Leap State Park, CT DEEP, 178 Still River Drive; open daily 8 a.m. to sunset, free; a walk-in park with about four miles of trails.',
  [W('Lovers Leap State Park'),W('Lover\'s Leap Bridge'),L('CT State Parks: Lovers Leap State Park','https://ctparks.com/parks/lovers-leap-state-park')],conf='mixed'),
N('cunningham-tower','Cunningham Tower','Cornwall','ct','landmark','ruins',41.82743,-73.30785,'exact','public','walk','','1913–',
  'The Mohawk Tower Association put up a wooden lookout tower on Mohawk Mountain in 1882; by 1892 it was unsafe and it later fell down. Seymour Cunningham of Litchfield bought farms here from 1912 for sheep, took over the association, and built a round stone tower, called the Aerie, in its place. The sheep farm failed, the land went to Alain White, and the family gave it to the state in 1921 (the account the Litchfield Historical Society gives, quoted by Damned CT).',
  'A gutted stone tower: roof, upper floor and stairs gone, steel beams, a tiled floor, a big fireplace and a carved ram\'s head spout high on the wall, as seen in 2015. It has been vandalised.',
  'A gentleman farmer\'s lookout tower, gutted in the woods.',
  'Mohawk State Park and Forest, CT DEEP; open daily April to November, 8 a.m. to sunset. On the blue-blazed Mohawk and Mattatuck trails in the northern section of the forest; the park road lets you park a short walk from it (Damned CT). The gates are on Great Hollow Road, off Route 4.',
  [W('Mohawk State Forest'),L('CT State Parks: Mohawk State Park/Forest','https://ctparks.com/parks/mohawk-state-parkforest'),L('Damned CT: Cunningham Tower','https://www.damnedct.com/cunningham-tower-cornwall/')]),
N('camp-columbia','Camp Columbia','Morris','ct','inst','ruins',41.67694,-73.22389,'site','public','walk','','1903–1983',
  'Columbia University\'s field camp for engineering and surveying students on Bantam Lake. The start date is given two ways: Wikipedia says the university bought the farmland in 1903; ConnecticutHistory.org says classes ran from 1885, on rented land at first, with purchases from 1903. Officers trained here in 1917 and dug trenches. A stone dining hall came in 1934 and a 60-foot stone water tower with a lookout, the gift of the Class of 1906, in 1942. The camp closed in 1983; in 1989 the town burned several decaying buildings in a fire-training exercise, and the state bought the land in 2000 and removed most of the rest.',
  'Building foundations and clearings, trench remains, the stone tower and the Instrument House. Sources differ on what else stands: ConnecticutHistory.org (2014) also names the boathouse; Wikipedia names only the tower and the Instrument House.',
  'A university summer campus left to the woods.',
  'Camp Columbia State Park and State Forest, CT DEEP. Parking in a grassy lot beside Route 109 (West Street). Hours were not checked, and whether the tower may be climbed was not confirmed: a hiker reported it officially closed in 2011 and open in 2014. Hunting is allowed in the state forest part in season.',
  [W('Camp Columbia State Park/State Forest'),L('ConnecticutHistory.org: Hidden Nearby: Camp Columbia State Park in Morris','https://connecticuthistory.org/hidden-nearby-camp-columbia-state-park-in-morris'),L('CTMQ: Camp Columbia tower','https://ctmq.org/camp-columbia-tower')],conf='mixed'),
N('leatherman-cave-watertown','Leatherman\'s Cave (Mattatuck)','Watertown','ct','landmark','traces',41.63939,-73.08794,'site','public','hike','','c.1857–1889',
  'The Leatherman, a vagabond in a home-made leather suit, walked the same loop between the Connecticut and Hudson rivers from about 1857 to 1889, returning to each town roughly every 34 days and sleeping in rock shelters along the way. He died in 1889 in a shelter in Mount Pleasant, New York. Who he was is still unknown.',
  'A shelter among big boulders on the blue-blazed Mattatuck Trail below Crane\'s Lookout, long called the Leatherman\'s Cave; the trail goes through it. Which shelter he really used is disputed: Wikipedia puts the Leatherman\'s Cave in Thomaston, a quarter mile west of the Jericho Trail junction, and names the formation at Crane\'s Lookout the Rock House; CTMQ says others point to a shelter off Park Road or on a feeder trail near Route 6. No structure: it is natural rock.',
  'A hermit legend\'s rock shelter, and an argument over which one.',
  'Mattatuck State Forest, CT DEEP. The Mattatuck Trail runs north to Bidwell Hill Road, where it enters Black Rock State Park (2065 Thomaston Road; open daily 8 a.m. to sunset). The shelter off Park Road is beside posted private land: stay on the path.',
  [W('Leatherman (vagabond)'),W('Mattatuck State Forest'),L('CTMQ: The Rock House (Leatherman\'s Cave at Crane\'s Lookout)','https://ctmq.org/leathermans-cave-watertown'),L('CTMQ: Leatherman\'s Cave via Park Road','https://ctmq.org/leathermans-cave-v-park-road'),L('CT State Parks: Black Rock State Park','https://ctparks.com/parks/black-rock-state-park')],conf='mixed'),
N('bear-mountain-monument','Bear Mountain stone monument','Salisbury','ct','landmark','ruins',42.04499,-73.45459,'exact','public','hike','','1885',
  'In 1885 the mason Owen Travis built a dry-stone pyramid on the summit, 22.5 feet tall, with a plaque calling it the highest ground in Connecticut. In the 1940s the USGS found that the state\'s highest ground is on the slope of Mount Frissell nearby, in Massachusetts. The monument was patched in 1972 and again by scouts, and in 1978 its east corner gave way and it collapsed. The state paid to stabilise what was left in 1982, and it was rebuilt in its present form in the fall of 1983.',
  'A lower, flat-topped stone pyramid that hikers use as a lookout, with the 1885 plaque and its wrong claim.',
  'The monument to a summit that turned out not to be the highest.',
  'On the Appalachian Trail; the National Park Service was named as owner in 1984. Start from the Undermountain Trail parking area on Route 41, 3.2 miles north of the centre of Salisbury (Mount Riga State Park, no fee), and climb by the Undermountain Trail and the AT.',
  [W('Bear Mountain (Connecticut)'),L('WillhiteWeb: Bear Mountain Tower','https://willhiteweb.com/appalachian_trail/fire_lookouts/bear_mountain_tower/connecticut_051.htm'),L('AMC Connecticut: History of the AT','https://ct-amc.org/?p=898'),DEEP_OTHER],
  th=[42.02889,-73.42904],thn='Undermountain Trail parking, Route 41'),
# ---- the Shepaug valley ----
N('steep-rock-tunnel','Shepaug railroad tunnel, Steep Rock','Washington','ct','rail','standing',41.60315,-73.33636,'site','public','walk','','1872–1948',
  'The Shepaug Valley Railroad, chartered in 1868 and opened in 1872, wound up the Shepaug valley to Litchfield. A crew of coal miners from Pennsylvania cut this curved tunnel through Steep Rock ridge by hand in nine months in 1871–72. The New Haven ran the line as its Litchfield branch from 1898; passenger service ended by 1932, freight in 1948, and the rails came up in 1949.',
  'A rough, curved tunnel of 235 feet cut through bedrock, dark inside, with the old rail bed as a trail on either side.',
  'A hand-blasted railroad tunnel, now a footpath.',
  'Steep Rock Preserve, Steep Rock Association; open sunrise to sunset. Main parking lot at 2 Tunnel Road, Washington Depot, next to the riding ring; take the yellow circle trail along Tunnel Road, then the blue square trail to the tunnel. Dogs on leash; no drones; groups of 15 or more need a permit.',
  [W('Shepaug, Litchfield and Northern Railroad'),L('Steep Rock Association: Steep Rock Preserve','https://steeprockassoc.org/preserve/steep-rock-preserve/')]),
N('holiday-house-steep-rock','Holiday House ruins, Steep Rock','Washington','ct','hotel','ruins',41.62071,-73.32146,'area','public','walk','','1893–1918',
  'Around 1892 Edward Van Ingen, a wool importer and the first New Yorker to build a big summer house in Washington, asked the architect Ehrick Rossiter to design a hotel on the hillside above the Shepaug, in memory of his eldest daughter, who had died of scarlet fever. Holiday House opened in 1893 as a holiday retreat for working women connected with Saint Bartholomew\'s Church in New York. The railroad had a flag stop, Valley Station, for it from 1893 to 1918.',
  'What remains of the house. Its extent was not described in the sources read: not checked.',
  '',
  'Steep Rock Preserve, Steep Rock Association; open sunrise to sunset. From the main parking lot at 2 Tunnel Road take the blue circle trail.',
  [L('Steep Rock Association: Steep Rock Preserve','https://steeprockassoc.org/preserve/steep-rock-preserve/'),W('Shepaug, Litchfield and Northern Railroad')],conf='mixed'),
]
BATCH={'key':'ctnw','label':'Northwest Connecticut: iron country and lost camps','added':'2026-11-17 05:00'}
WKA='Pin is the coordinate given in the Wikipedia article.'
